{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 55, :comments ["<b>To the names of the tribes of their fathers.</b> These are those who came out of Egypt. Scripture changed this inheritance from all inheritances that are in the Torah — in all inheritances the living inherit from the dead, but here the dead inherit from the living. How? Two brothers among those who came out of Egypt, who had sons upon entry to the land — to one, one son; to the other, three. The one took one portion and the three took three, as it says ״לָאֵלֶּה תֵּחָלֵק הָאָרֶץ״. Their inheritance reverted to their father's father and they divided everything equally. And this is what is said: <b>to the names of the tribes of their fathers they shall inherit</b> — that after the sons took, they divided it according to the fathers who came out of Egypt. And had they divided it initially by the number of those who came out of Egypt, these four would have taken only two portions; now they took four portions. (Sifrei; Bava Batra 117)" "<b>Only by lot.</b> Joshua and Caleb went out [were excluded], and so it says ״וַיִּתְּנוּ לְכָלֵב אֶת חֶבְרוֹן כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה״ (Judges 1), and it says (Joshua 19) ״עַל פִּי ה׳ נָתְנוּ לוֹ אֶת הָעִיר אֲשֶׁר שָׁאָל״. (Sifrei)" "<b>Tribes of their fathers.</b> Converts and slaves went out [were excluded]."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:00.169876574Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}